If you'd rather keep `.shell` as an empty hook, only the `container-type` line would go.

## Places where the shell renders narrower than the viewport

I found no HTML or JS that renders the shell. The repo has only CSS, `.mjs` modules and tests. These are the places that say it is embedded:

- `src/layout/shell.css:1` has the comment "The shell is also rendered inside split panes."
- `README.md:7` says "`src/layout/` — app shell CSS (the shell is also embedded in split panes)".
- `src/layout/PRECEDENT.md:9` says the panes go down to 380px on a 1440px screen.

The shell's container width can differ from the viewport width. With a `@media (min-width: 720px)` rule, a 380px pane on a 1440px screen would get `grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr`. That leaves about 140px for the content column and 24px padding, which is the sidebar-clipping incident the record describes.

## Tests, lint and build

I didn't run anything, because no files changed. The repo has `npm test` (`node --test`), but its tests only cover `src/auth` and `src/tabs`, nothing in CSS. There is no lint or build script. `scripts/check.sh` is an advisory pre-commit check for large staged files.

## Decision for the user

Tell me whether you want to override `§container-not-viewport`, naming the decision. If so, I'll apply the diff above. I'd also rewrite the PRECEDENT entry with today's date (2026-10-23) and a note that the user overrode it. Otherwise, if the team's concern is that container queries are unfamiliar, a short comment above the `@container` block explaining it would address that without reintroducing the clipping bug.
